---
// Continuous drift detection: the engine watches between plans. Placed right after WhyEngine on
// purpose: that band says Turf is a service where the old tool is a process that exits, and this
// is the first thing only a service can do. Its run timeline is "one run"; this figure is
// "between runs". The headline is "On watch, between plans." (2026-11-16: keeping watch, and
// `watch` is the tool's name); it names no one's mark, and "Terraform" is only ever used
// nominatively (the history is in CLAUDE.md, "Trademark"). The headline carries "between plans",
// so the lede's second sentence does not repeat it. Three standing rules for this band
// (2026-11-16; don't drop one): Turf is the subject of the lede ("Turf brings continuous drift detection to …", then "Its engine …");
// the band head carries the non-affiliation sentence as fine print under the lede, word for word
// as CompatibilityTeaser.astro has it; and the Terraform chip's swatch in the grid is the site's
// own ink, never HashiCorp's brand purple. The lede still carries the phrase people search for, and says plainly that this
// is the engine keeping watch, not a plan on a schedule. Rules kept: present tense; no number or speed claim ("as it happens",
// "between plans"); "GitOps controllers" is the generic term and none is named; the engine
// detects and notifies, and never applies on its own; Helm releases are caught by the sweep (the
// provider's own read), so no watch is claimed for them. The exchange is illustrative, in the
// shape of the MCP server's `watch` result (address, class, paths, source; never a value); a
// change the sweep finds carries the source "sweep". The sweep is the signal that ships; the
// sensor for Kubernetes clusters is coming soon (2026-11-26) and carries the pill in the ticklist.
// A console still of the Drift tab belongs under the figure once one is taken from a real run;
// the band reads complete without it.
import DriftFigure from './figures/DriftFigure.astro';
---

<section class="band alt" id="drift">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>On watch, between plans.</h2>
      <p><strong>Turf brings continuous drift detection to everything a Terraform provider manages.</strong>
        Its engine keeps watch and wakes your agent only when something really changed. Not a
        scheduled plan: a change made outside Turf arrives as a drift notice, and the
        fix is a plan like any other.</p>
      <p class="fine">Turf is an independent product built on the OpenTofu framework, not affiliated
        with or endorsed by HashiCorp.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="figwide">
      <DriftFigure />
      <p class="figcap"><b>Between runs.</b> The sweep only reads, a notice names what changed, and a
        change still takes a plan and an approval.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature">
      <div>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>A paced sweep</b> <span>— the engine reads every managed object,
            of any provider, on a cycle you set. <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span> a sensor
            that reports changes in a Kubernetes® cluster as they happen.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Signal, not noise</b> <span>— each read is classified against your
            Terraform configuration before anything reaches your agent. Status churn, Turf’s own
            applies and ignored fields wake nobody; a field another controller keeps setting is named
            as contended.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>The plan still governs</b> <span>— the agent decides which side
            wins: remediate, adopt the change into the files, or suppress it with
            <code>ignore_changes</code>. The plan reads only what drifted and stops at the same approval
            points. Nothing runs off-plan.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Beyond the cluster</b> <span>— what GitOps controllers do for
            Kubernetes manifests, for the rest of the stack: cloud resources, DNS, Helm® releases, the
            clusters themselves.</span></div></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="proof">
          <span class="lab">More</span>
          <a href="/docs/concepts/drift/">how drift is detected →</a>
          <a href="/docs/using/drift/">watching a workspace →</a>
          <a href="/engine/#drift">in the engine →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="agentloop" role="img" aria-label="A tool result: watch returns one drift notice, the replica count of a Kubernetes deployment, class configured, found by the sweep. The agent plans only what drifted, reads one object, finds one in-place change back to what the files say, approves it, and the notice clears.">
<pre class="report"><span class="lab">TOOL RESULT · TURF MCP</span><b>watch</b> → <i>drift</i>
<span class="k">notices</span> = [&#123;
  address = <i>"kubernetes_deployment_v1.api"</i>
  class   = <i>"configured"</i>
  paths   = [<i>"spec[0].replicas"</i>]
  source  = <i>"sweep"</i>
&#125;]
<span class="d">confirmed by one read · next: up</span></pre>
        <div class="chat">
          <div class="who"><span class="av">✦</span><span>Your agent</span><span class="tag">in Claude Code</span></div>
          <div class="msg">
            <p><strong>The replica count on api was changed outside Turf.</strong> The files say three,
              so I planned only what drifted.</p>
            <ul class="ck">
              <li><code>plan</code> with <code>refresh_drift</code> <span>1 read</span></li>
              <li>1 to change, in place <span>5 → 3 replicas</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p>An in-place revert to what the files say: approved.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="note">→ apply(plan_digest): converged · the notice clears</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
